Derive the following The Joule-Thomson Effect, Throttling, and Inversion Temperature relation from the preceding result, showing the algebra, calculus, counting, or thermodynamic identity used at each step.
\[
\mu_{JT}
=\frac{1}{C_P}
\left[T\left(\frac{\partial V_m}{\partial T}\right)_p-V_m\right]
=\frac{V_m}{C_P}(\alpha_pT-1).
\tag*{[3]}
\]
Notation: \(p,\Delta p,T,\Delta T,V_m\) denotes pressure and its finite change, absolute temperature and its finite change, and molar volume; unit \(\mathrm{Pa, Pa, K, K, m^{3} mol^{-1}}\); \(H,H_m\) denotes total and molar enthalpy; unit \(\mathrm{J, J mol^{-1}}\); \(C_P\) denotes molar heat capacity at constant pressure; unit \(\mathrm{J mol^{-1} K^{-1}}\); \(\mu_{JT}\) denotes Joule-Thomson coefficient at constant enthalpy and fixed amount; unit \(\mathrm{K Pa^{-1}}\); \(\alpha_p\) denotes isobaric volumetric expansion coefficient; unit \(\mathrm{K^{-1}}\); \(a,b,R\) denotes van der Waals attraction parameter, excluded molar volume, and molar gas constant; unit \(\mathrm{Pa m^{6} mol^{-2}, m^{3} mol^{-1}, J mol^{-1} K^{-1}}\); \(T_i\) denotes inversion temperature in the low-pressure approximation; unit \(\mathrm{K}\).

